00:00:09.871 --> 00:00:10.724
Part 1.

5
00:00:11.004 --> 00:00:11.900
Problem.

6
00:00:13.150 --> 00:00:17.438
Count unique daily visitors across a billion events on many servers.

7
00:00:17.718 --> 00:00:26.251
Use kilobytes instead of gigabytes, accept about 1% error, and be able to merge counts from different servers or days without double counting.

8
00:00:27.601 --> 00:00:28.476
Part 2.

9
00:00:28.756 --> 00:00:29.673
Key idea.

10
00:00:30.923 --> 00:00:32.075
Hash each item.

11
00:00:32.355 --> 00:00:35.854
The first p bits choose one of m = 2^p registers.

12
00:00:36.134 --> 00:00:47.099
Each register keeps the maximum position of the first 1-bit seen in the remaining bits, because a run of k leading zeros happens about once per 2^k distinct items.

13
00:00:47.379 --> 00:00:53.481
The estimate is a bias-corrected harmonic mean over the registers, with linear counting for small sets.

14
00:00:53.761 --> 00:00:56.214
A merge is the register-wise maximum.

15
00:00:56.494 --> 00:01:06.179
The standard error is 1.04 / √m, about 0.81% for m = 16,384.

16
00:01:07.529 --> 00:01:08.873
Here is the diagram.

17
