101
00:07:57.842 --> 00:07:58.717
Part 8.

102
00:07:58.997 --> 00:08:00.106
Common mistakes.

103
00:08:01.356 --> 00:08:04.108
A weak hash (for example String.hashCode).

104
00:08:04.388 --> 00:08:08.036
HyperLogLog depends on uniformly distributed bits.

105
00:08:08.316 --> 00:08:10.172
Adding counts from different servers.

106
00:08:10.452 --> 00:08:12.564
That double counts users seen by both.

107
00:08:12.844 --> 00:08:14.444
Merge the sketches instead.

108
00:08:14.724 --> 00:08:16.985
Expecting intersections to be accurate.

109
00:08:17.265 --> 00:08:23.921
Inclusion-exclusion on HLLs has large errors; use MinHash or Theta sketches for intersections.

110
00:08:25.271 --> 00:08:26.167
Part 9.

111
00:08:26.447 --> 00:08:27.791
Follow-up questions.

112
00:08:29.041 --> 00:08:29.916
Question.

113
00:08:30.196 --> 00:08:32.969
How does Redis store HLL in 12 KB?

114
00:08:33.249 --> 00:08:34.615
Thirty-second answer:

115
00:08:34.895 --> 00:08:42.063
16,384 registers times 6 bits = 12 KB in the dense encoding.

116
00:08:42.343 --> 00:08:49.383
Small cardinalities use a sparse run-length encoding of a few hundred bytes, switching to dense as the count grows.

117
00:08:50.733 --> 00:08:51.607
Question.

118
00:08:51.887 --> 00:08:54.234
Where would you use HyperLogLog in a design?

119
00:08:54.514 --> 00:08:55.879
Thirty-second answer:

120
00:08:56.159 --> 00:09:08.554
Unique visitors per page per day, distinct search queries, unique IPs per minute for DDoS detection, and cardinality estimates in query planners (Presto, BigQuery APPROXCOUNTDISTINCT).

